WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.940
What if you have a kinase but do not know the substrates?

2
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.640
The approaches each have a weakness.

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:17.460
A purified kinase in a test tube shows what it can phosphorylate, which is more than

4
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:18.460
it does in a cell.

5
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.840
Prediction from sequence gives candidates.

6
00:00:22.783 --> 00:00:28.483
Finding the kinase bound to a protein shows contact, which is not catalysis.

7
00:00:29.643 --> 00:00:35.303
Removing the kinase with an inhibitor, RNA interference or a knockout changes many

8
00:00:35.303 --> 00:00:40.623
proteins. The direct substrates look the same as those three steps downstream.

9
00:00:41.517 --> 00:00:47.637
Kevan Shokat's laboratory found a way through, which Chris introduces as organic

10
00:00:47.637 --> 00:00:50.097
chemistry meets protein manipulation.

11
00:00:51.397 --> 00:00:53.657
Kinases share an ATP pocket.

12
00:00:54.357 --> 00:00:58.357
One bulky residue in it, the gatekeeper, limits what fits.

13
00:00:59.357 --> 00:01:03.997
Replace the gatekeeper with glycine or alanine, and the pocket gains a hole.

14
00:01:04.917 --> 00:01:11.517
An ATP with a bulky group added to its adenine fits the enlarged pocket, and fits

15
00:01:11.517 --> 00:01:13.097
normal kinases poorly.

16
00:01:14.037 --> 00:01:16.477
Such a kinase is called analog-sensitive.

17
00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:22.957
The laboratory introduced the approach in 1997 with the tyrosine kinase v-Src.
